      <h2 id="ladder">The three-bottle ladder</h2>
      <p>Baijiu is a category, not a product. Buying one bottle and deciding you do or do not like baijiu is like buying one IPA and deciding about beer. The efficient approach is three bottles across three aroma types, tasted side by side. Total cost is roughly $60, which is less than one mid-tier Moutai.</p>

      <h3>Bottle 1 — Light aroma: <a href="/db/variety/fenjiu/">Fenjiu</a></h3>
      <p><strong>Around $15–40 · 42% or 53% · Shanxi · GB/T 10781.2</strong></p>
      <p>This is the cleanest expression of baijiu and the easiest to read for a palate trained on Western spirits. Expect steamed rice, pear, a faint floral lift, and a dry finish that stops rather than lingers. Nothing here is challenging, which is the point: it establishes the baseline of what grain spirit tastes like before fermentation character is layered on.</p>
      <p>Choose the 42% if you are completely new to high-proof spirits. Choose the 53% if you drink cask-strength whisky or overproof rum and want the standard expression. Either way, Fenjiu is one of the best-value bottles in the entire category — its flagship costs less than a mid-range wine and outperforms bottles at three times the price.</p>
      <p><em>What it teaches:</em> what clean grain distillation tastes like, and how much aroma comes from fermentation rather than the still.</p>

      <h3>Bottle 2 — Soft strong aroma: <a href="/db/variety/yanghe/">Yanghe</a></h3>
      <p><strong>Around $30–50 · 40.8% · Jiangsu · GB/T 10781.1</strong></p>
      <p>Strong aroma is roughly half of all baijiu sold, so you cannot skip it. But the Sichuan versions at 52% are a lot to handle on a second bottle. Yanghe solves this: it is a deliberate soft style — the industry calls it <em>mianrou</em> (绵柔, "soft and supple") — at 40.8%, which is low for the category.</p>
      <p>You get the characteristic strong-aroma fruit and honey, plus a rounder, gentler body. It is also the most food-friendly baijiu made: it was designed for the long, delicate Huaiyang meals of Jiangsu, and it works with seafood and light dishes where a 52% Sichuan bottle would dominate.</p>
      <p><em>What it teaches:</em> the strong-aroma signature — fruit, honey, cellar — at a strength you can evaluate calmly. Once you know it, a 52% Wuliangye makes sense as the louder version of the same idea.</p>

      <h3>Bottle 3 — Sauce aroma: <a href="/db/variety/xijiu/">Xijiu</a> or <a href="/db/variety/langjiu/">Langjiu</a></h3>
      <p><strong>Around $30–60 · 53% · Guizhou / Sichuan · GB/T 26760</strong></p>
      <p>Sauce aroma is the style everyone has heard of, and you should try it — just not through a $150 Moutai bottle. Xijiu and Langjiu are serious sauce-aroma producers with decades of history, and both cost a fraction of Moutai while showing the style honestly.</p>
      <p>Expect fermented soybean, roasted grain, dried mushroom, toasted nuts, and a long savoury finish. This is the bottle that divides people. If you love peated Islay whisky, mezcal, or funky rum, you may find it immediately compelling. If you prefer clean spirits, you may find it medicinal — and that is a legitimate reaction, not a failure of palate.</p>
      <p><em>What it teaches:</em> whether sauce aroma is for you. That answer is worth $40, not $150.</p>

