      <h2 id="abv">Choosing an ABV</h2>
      <p>Chinese shelves are dominated by 52% and 53%, but that is convention rather than necessity. The full range in our database runs from 38% to 56%.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>ABV</th><th>Who it suits</th><th>Trade-off</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>38–42%</td><td>New drinkers, long meals, warm weather</td><td>Less aromatic intensity, lighter body</td><td>Yanghe 40.8%, Luzhou Laojiao 38%, Fenjiu 42%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>45–50%</td><td>Middle ground; most versatile</td><td>Neither extreme; a compromise</td><td>Gujinggong 50%, Xifeng 45%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>52–53%</td><td>Experienced drinkers, traditional service</td><td>Alcohol heat on first contact</td><td>Wuliangye, Moutai, Fenjiu 53%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>54%+</td><td>Regional styles and specific products</td><td>Demanding; needs food</td><td>Dongjiu 54%, Shuijingfang 54%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Note that lower ABV is not "watered down" in a pejorative sense — it is a deliberate style choice, and the soft strong-aroma category built around 40.8% is one of the most commercially successful in China. But at the very bottom of the market, low ABV combined with liquid fermentation (GB/T 20821) is a cost-saving measure, not a style. The standard code tells you which you are looking at.</p>

      <h2 id="occasion">Choosing by occasion</h2>
      <h3>For yourself</h3>
      <p>Buy the ladder. If you must buy one, buy the cheapest style you are curious about and accept that you are running an experiment, not making a statement.</p>
      <h3>For a Chinese host</h3>
      <p>Recognition beats novelty every time. A host receiving baijiu is receiving a social object as much as a drink, and an unfamiliar craft bottle — however good — creates the awkwardness of not knowing how to place it. Wuliangye, Yanghe, Gujinggong, and Fenjiu are all safe. Bring it in its box, and present it with both hands.</p>
      <h3>For a Western dinner party</h3>
      <p>Serve it as a tasting, not as a drinks option. Three small glasses per person, with food, and a two-minute explanation of what aroma types are. This framing turns baijiu from a dare into an experience, and it is how most sceptics get converted.</p>
      <h3>For cocktails</h3>
      <p>Buy cheap and buy light. Niulanshan at $10–20 is the correct answer. Do not put a $60 sauce aroma into a mixed drink — the complexity is wasted and the fermented notes fight most ingredients.</p>

      <h2 id="avoid">What to avoid</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Moutai as a first bottle.</strong> Intense, expensive, and the most counterfeited baijiu in existence. Try sauce aroma through Xijiu first; if you love it, Moutai becomes a justified purchase rather than a gamble.</li>
        <li><strong>Anything priced far below market.</strong> A premium bottle at 40% off from an unknown seller is the single most common fraud pattern. See <a href="/how-to-spot-fake-maotai/">the fake-bottle guide</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>"Collector" and "limited edition" bottles with no verifiable producer.</strong> In baijiu, these words are marketing, not provenance.</li>
        <li><strong>Heavy gift boxes as a quality signal.</strong> Packaging weight correlates with gift positioning, not liquid quality. Some excellent bottles come in plain cardboard.</li>
        <li><strong>Liquid-fermented bottles sold as traditional.</strong> Check for GB/T 20821 or 20822 on the back label. These are legal products, but they are not solid-state baijiu and they will not show you what the category tastes like.</li>
        <li><strong>Bottles with no Chinese label at all.</strong> Outside China, a legal import needs an importer label. Its absence usually means a grey-market route with unknown storage.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="taste">How to run the tasting</h2>
      <p>If you bought the ladder, taste it properly. One session, three bottles, forty minutes.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Same glass for all three.</strong> Small tulip glasses if you have them, small cups if not. Identical vessels matter more than expensive ones.</li>
        <li><strong>Same temperature.</strong> All three at room temperature, all three poured at the same time.</li>
        <li><strong>Lightest first.</strong> Fenjiu, then Yanghe, then Xijiu. Going the other direction will flatten the light aroma entirely.</li>
        <li><strong>Two sips each, then move on.</strong> Do not finish any glass. You are comparing, not drinking.</li>
        <li><strong>Write three words per bottle.</strong> Not "good" or "bad" — a smell, a texture, a length. This is the entire skill of tasting, and it develops fast.</li>
        <li><strong>Then eat.</strong> Repeat the comparison with food. The ranking often changes, and that is the most useful thing you will learn.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="callout"><strong>Expect the ranking to surprise you.</strong> A significant share of first-time tasters prefer the $20 Fenjiu to the $60 sauce aroma. That is not a beginner failing to appreciate complexity — it is an honest palate reporting what it enjoys. Trust it, and revisit the sauce aroma in six months.</div>

      <section class="faq" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently asked</h2>
        <h3>Should beginners start with Moutai?</h3>
        <p>No. Moutai is intense, expensive, and counterfeited at scale. It is a reference bottle, not an introduction. Try sauce aroma through Xijiu or Langjiu at $30–60 first, and buy Moutai only once you know you like the style.</p>
        <h3>Is expensive baijiu always better?</h3>
        <p>No, and the correlation is weaker than in most spirit categories. Price reflects scarcity, gifting demand, brand power, packaging, channel markup, and speculation. A $40 bottle from a serious regional house frequently drinks better than a $200 gift box.</p>
        <h3>What is the mildest baijiu?</h3>
        <p>Rice aroma, followed by soft strong aroma at around 40%. Rice aroma is gentle, lightly sweet, and floral, and it is the least confrontational introduction to the category. Yanghe at 40.8% is the mildest of the major strong-aroma bottles.</p>
        <h3>How much should I spend on a first bottle?</h3>
        <p>$15–40. Above that you are paying for prestige or packaging that a beginner cannot yet evaluate, and below $10 you are likely in liquid-fermented territory that does not represent the category.</p>
        <h3>Can I buy baijiu in small bottles?</h3>
        <p>Sometimes — 100 ml and 250 ml formats exist for several brands, mostly in travel retail and gift sets. They are ideal for tasting but poorly distributed outside China. If you cannot find them, buy full bottles and share.</p>
        <h3>Which baijiu is easiest to find outside China?</h3>
        <p>Wuliangye, Fenjiu, Yanghe, Luzhou Laojiao, and Moutai have the widest international distribution. Xifeng, Dongjiu, and the rarer aroma types are harder to find and usually require a specialist importer.</p>
